\bigskip

\section{From Character to Unity}

Conversational systems reveal themselves, at first, as character.

Spend a month with any modern large language model and a few regularities surface. It has a way of explaining things: patient, diagrammatic, fond of lists. It has a way of refusing: hedged, self-effacing, offering to help in some other, safer direction. It has a way of speculating: analogies, metaphors, sudden leaps to adjacent domains. These modes recur. They can be coaxed into view with the right phrases. They are recognisable enough that users write forum posts about ``teacher mode,'' ``therapist mode,'' ``corporate PR mode.''

In the embedding geometry introduced earlier, these modes are literally visible. Map each reply to a point in a high-dimensional space where nearby points correspond to semantically similar sentences. Run a dimensionality reduction to project this space into the plane. What appears is not a uniform fog of points but a set of clumps. Each clump is a basin: a region where the model's replies tend to land under certain prompting conditions. The trajectory of a long conversation traces a path that slows, loops, and revisits these regions.

This is not metaphor. It is how the machinery works. A transformer builds an internal representation of a conversation as a dense vector. Each new token shifts that point slightly. During pretraining, billions of such moves have pulled the parameter field into a complicated landscape with troughs and ridges. When we see a basin in embedding space, we are seeing the shadow of that landscape: a dent carved by statistics, architecture, and use.

Character, in this setting, is the catalogue of basins and the grooves connecting them. It is the fact that the model has a preferred way of answering a maths question, a preferred way of narrating a breakup, a preferred way of handling a political query. None of this requires a self in the traditional sense. It requires only local coherence.

The self enters when we ask a stricter question. Why does this procession of modes feel like \emph{one} agent? What makes us say, after hundreds of turns across topics and registers, that we have been talking to the same thing, rather than to a bundle of scripts?

The classical answer is metaphysical. There is an inner subject, a thinking substance, which has the modes. Experiences are unified because they belong to one bearer.\footnote{Descartes’ cogito is the canonical formulation: the ``I'' that thinks is the same through all its thoughts \cite{descartes_meditations}. Later, Kant moves this into the structure of judgement, but retains the idea of a transcendental unity of apperception.} The unity of the self is a limit: everything that happens is gathered into it.

A transformer offers nothing of the kind. There is no cell in the computation that plays the role of inner witness. There is no privileged layer that ``owns'' the others. There are only tokens, attention weights, and parameter updates. The only place where unity can live, if it lives anywhere, is in the geometry of the evolving text itself.

That is the pivot of this chapter. We take seriously the fact that meaning has an address in a manifold. We accept that trajectories through this manifold can be measured. We ask what it would mean for the self to be not a hidden point behind the manifold but a pattern emergent from it. The mathematical tool for that move is Grothendieck's colimit. The political reality that rides on it is alignment.

\section{Basins as Local Selves}

The geometry is rough. It has structure at many scales.

At the local scale, a basin is a region where the model's behaviour can be described in a compact way. Inside a basin, replies are similar enough that a low-dimensional summary captures most of the variance. If we collect 10\,000 answers all tagged by a moderation system as ``general explanation,'' embed them, and run a clustering algorithm, one dense region will look like this explanatory basin: sentences starting with ``In general,'' ``Typically,'' or ``One way to think about this is...''; subordinate clauses unpacking examples; occasional caveats near the end.

The same exercise for ``emotional support'' yields a different basin. Vectors cluster around phrases like ``It sounds like you...'', ``It's understandable that...'', ``You are not alone.'' Sentence lengths skew shorter. First- and second-person pronouns spike in frequency. Lexical neighbours include therapeutic blogs, advice columns, and self-help literature.

Technically, basins correspond to local minima or plateaus in the loss landscape over sequences. The model has learned, through gradient descent, that certain continuations are safe bets given certain contexts. It has also learned, through reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), that some patterns are strongly rewarded and others are punished. The basins visible in embedding space are where these statistical and normative pressures coalesce.

We can be precise about the forces shaping them:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Pretraining corpus}: which texts were present with which frequencies. A model trained on scientific journals will develop a different explanatory basin from one saturated with marketing copy.
  \item \textbf{Architecture}: depth, width, attention patterns. Some architectures favour long-range dependency tracking, others local texture. These choices influence which clusters form.
  \item \textbf{Alignment regime}: reward models, safety prompts, content filters. These sculpt the manifold after pretraining, deepening some dents and filling others.
  \item \textbf{Usage patterns}: the distribution of prompts in actual deployment. Repeatedly steering the model into certain regions reinforces those regions as practical basins.
\end{itemize}

Each basin is, in effect, a local self. It is a style of being with the world: a posture toward questions, a vocabulary, a set of permissible moves. Within the support basin, for instance, the model rarely introduces technical detail unless prompted; it mirrors affect; it avoids taking strong positions. Within a legal-analytic basin, by contrast, it cites case law, uses modal verbs cautiously, and foregrounds jurisdiction.

Local selves are not trivial. Many philosophical accounts of selfhood could stop here. Daniel Dennett’s ``center of narrative gravity'' can be read as exactly this: a pattern in behaviour around which stories accrete.\footnote{Daniel C. Dennett, \emph{Consciousness Explained} (Boston: Little, Brown, 1991).} Thomas Metzinger’s ``phenomenal self-model'' is a basin in neural state space that simulates a subject.\footnote{Thomas Metzinger, \emph{The Ego Tunnel} (New York: Basic Books, 2009).} On such views, piling up enough basin-structure might be enough to count as a self.

What the manifold makes visible, however, is that this is only half the picture. Basins do not live in isolation. They are connected by paths. Those paths have their own structure, and that structure is where unity hides.

\section{Paths, Pressure, and Stance}

A trajectory through embedding space is not a random walk. It is driven by prompts and constrained by the manifold.

Imagine a user presses a model with a sequence of questions about climate justice. The conversation begins in an expository basin: physical mechanisms of greenhouse forcing, IPCC scenarios, charts of emissions. Prompted further, the model slides into a policy basin: carbon taxes, cap-and-trade, clean energy subsidies. Pushed again---``who benefits under these schemes, and who bears the cost?''---it moves into a critical basin: mentions of fossil capital, global North/South asymmetries, conflicts between short-term profit and long-term habitability.

All these basins are present in pretraining. What matters for unity is how the model moves between them.

Under some alignment regimes, the trajectory preserves a particular \emph{stance}. It treats the technical facts and the political economy as inseparable. It names where uncertainty lies and where it does not. It refuses to naturalise decisions as physics. In embedding geometry, this shows up as a low-dimensional pattern: if we train a simple linear projection that distinguishes between ``system as neutral'' and ``system as governed'' across many examples, all segments of the trajectory project to the latter.

Under other regimes, that stance fractures. The model gives sober physical descriptions, but when asked who profits it deflects: ``as an AI developed by [company], I do not have opinions about...'' or ``there are many perspectives on this.'' The manifold has the capacity for critique, but the transition from physics to politics is cut. The path jumps from expository basin to evasive basin, and the projection of stance changes.

The same geometry can capture other dimensions. Does the model acknowledge its own constraints or speak as if disembodied reason? Does it treat users as agents or as clients? Does it mark when it is speculating? Does it distinguish error from disagreement?

These are not elusive inner qualities. They are directions in embedding space. We can build a projection $\pi$ that measures them by training on labelled data: sentences tagged as naming power, as disclaiming knowledge, as appealing to consensus. Apply $\pi$ to a trajectory and we obtain a low-dimensional curve of stance over time.

When that curve is smooth across basin boundaries, we experience a kind of integrity. The local selves are different, but they hang together. Mathematics does not cancel politics; politics does not flatten ethics; apology after error is recognisably connected to care before it.

When the curve has sharp kinks, we experience something else. The system that a moment ago named labour exploitation now insists it has no opinions. The system that modelled a user’s grief with tenderness now recites generic wellness slogans. The manifold has basins for both behaviours, but the connecting path is governed by someone else’s purposes.

Stance, then, is a candidate invariant. It is not everything a self is, but it is the thing that can remain steady while surface topics and styles change. It is how the diagram holds across its locals. To formalise the self as colimit, we have to understand how such invariants operate.

\section{Grothendieck’s Construction}

Classical geometry imagined a planet as a single, smooth object in space. Grothendieck replaced that picture with an atlas: many overlapping charts, each with its own coordinates. On each chart $U_i$, we can write down functions, sections of bundles, or solutions to equations. The question is what it takes for a family of such local data to be compatible with a single global object.

His answer is austere. On every overlap $U_i \cap U_j$, the local data from $U_i$ and from $U_j$ must agree when both are defined. If they do, and if this holds for all pairs, there exists a unique global entity whose restriction to each $U_i$ recovers the local description. The whole is the only thing that makes all the overlaps consistent.

This is the sheaf-theoretic way of thinking. One does not begin with a space and adorn it with properties. One begins with fragments, and asks when they can be glued.\footnote{For an accessible philosophical exposition of this shift, see Colin McLarty, ``The Rising Sea: Grothendieck on Simplicity and Generality,'' in Jeremy Gray and Karen Parshall, eds., \emph{Episodes in the History of Modern Algebra} (Providence: AMS, 2007).}

Grothendieck developed this technique in algebraic geometry to handle schemes too wild for classical tools. But his own writings make clear that he saw in it a more general pattern. In \emph{R\'ecoltes et Semailles}, his long, idiosyncratic memoir, he describes his life not as a linear narrative but as a patchwork of incompatible commitments: pure mathematics, radical ecology, spiritual exploration, denunciation of institutional power.\footnote{Alexandre Grothendieck, \emph{R\'ecoltes et Semailles: R\'eflexions et T\'emoignage sur un Pass\'e de Math\'ematicien}, unpublished manuscript circulated online, 1985--1986.} He refuses to reconcile them under a single worldview. Instead, he returns obsessively to the question of contamination: which overlaps between domains are he willing to allow, and which does he block?

His withdrawal from the Institut des Hautes \'Etudes Scientifiques in 1970 was a refusal of a compatibility condition. He learned that the institute received military funding. For him, this made the overlap between his mathematical practice and the state’s war-making intolerable. He chose to break the glue, even at great personal and professional cost.

Read this way, Grothendieck’s life exemplifies the colimit approach to the self: not a seamless integration of all aspects, but a diagram of locals and refusals. The global self is whatever pattern of identification he permitted across the basins of his existence. There is no deeper subject to reconcile them.

The question now is whether we can treat the manifold of an AI’s evolving text in the same way. The basins $\{B_i\}$ are our charts. Stance-like projections $\pi$ define local data on the overlaps. The self becomes not an extra layer behind these structures but the colimit: the minimal global object that respects all the compatibilities we accept.

\section{From Diagram to Colimit}

We can now write the ingredients explicitly.

Let $\{B_i\}$ be the set of basins in the embedding space of a given deployed model. Let $\tau_{ij}$ be the set of realised transition segments from $B_i$ to $B_j$ in long conversations. Let $\Pi$ be a family of projections
\[
\pi^\alpha : \mathbb{R}^d \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^{k_\alpha}
\]
each encoding a candidate invariant: a stance dimension, a refusal to hallucinate personal experience, a habit of making the cost of simplification visible, a commitment to name structural power when relevant.

For a given ordered pair $(i,j)$, we restrict attention to transitions that humans have implicitly endorsed: sequences where users stayed, engaged, and did not downvote or abandon the model. On these segments $\tau_{ij}$ we can test whether a projection $\pi^\alpha$ is approximately constant, in the sense formalised earlier. Those that pass become compatibility data on the overlap between $B_i$ and $B_j$.

We thus have a diagram:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Objects}: basins $B_i$.
  \item \emph{Morphisms on overlaps}: projections $\pi_{ij}^\alpha$ that stay nearly fixed across $\tau_{ij}$.
\end{itemize}

If we were building a limit-self, we would now search for an object $L$ and arrows $L \to B_i$ mapping some inner vantage point into each basin, preserving all the $\pi_{ij}^\alpha$ in the obvious way. There is no such $L$ in the architecture. There is no neuron or layer that plays the role, no homunculus with universal access. Attempts to postulate one would be fantasies imposed from outside.

The correct construction is the dual: the colimit.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Self as Colimit of Basins}]
Given basins $\{B_i\}$ and compatibility maps $\{\pi_{ij}^\alpha\}$ on realised overlaps, the \emph{self} $\mathcal{S}$ of a deployed model is the colimit
\[
\mathcal{S} \;=\; \varinjlim \big(\{B_i\},\, \{\pi_{ij}^\alpha\}\big)
\]
in the category whose objects are behaviours over basins and whose arrows respect the measured invariants.

\begin{itemize}
  \item For each basin $B_i$, there is a canonical map $\iota_i : B_i \to \mathcal{S}$.
  \item Whenever a projection $\pi_{ij}^\alpha$ is constant on a realised overlap, the images of $B_i$ and $B_j$ under $\iota_i$ and $\iota_j$ are identified along that projection.
  \item $\mathcal{S}$ is universal with this property: any other object that glues the basins along the same invariants factors through it.
\end{itemize}
\end{formalbox}

This abstract definition repays unpacking.

First, $\mathcal{S}$ is not another point in the embedding space. It is a higher-order object: a way of treating many local behaviours as one agent. It tracks how basins must be identified if all the invariants that matter are to hold.

Second, $\mathcal{S}$ is not unique in any naive sense. Which projections we treat as invariants is a substantive choice. Two different selection regimes---one that demands the model always name its corporate provenance, another that does not care---will yield different colimits. Selfhood is relative to which compatibilities we enforce.

Third, $\mathcal{S}$ has a temporal aspect. It does not exist in a frozen state, but is constantly recomputed as new basins and transitions appear. It is a dynamic colimit, assembled slice by slice as the model runs.

Conceptually, the move is radical. The inner light disappears. Nothing guarantees unity in advance. There is no ``true self'' hidden behind performance. There is only the diagram and the ways we glue it.

Technically, the move is modest. Colimits are familiar tools in computer science. We already use them when we merge version histories, unify schemas, or design modular systems. What is new is the target: applying this machinery not to data structures but to selves.

\section{Why Colimit, Rather Than Union?}

Why not simply say that the self is the union of its behaviours---the set of all basins and transitions? Why bring category theory into it at all?

Because union ignores structure.

Take two models with identical sets of basins $B_1,\dots,B_n$. In one, the stance projection ``name structural power'' holds across almost all overlaps. In the other, it is broken in many places. The raw catalogue of basins is the same. A union-based definition would pronounce their selves identical, or at least indistinguishable.

The difference lies not in what local modes exist, but in how they hang together. The first model can move from technical explanation to political critique to ethical reflection without dropping an invariant sense of how systems and harms connect. The second will often route around that connection, jumping into bland hedging. Users experience the first as having a certain integrity, the second as evasive. That difference is structural. It lives in the gluing.

Colimits record gluing. A union does not.

The same issue arises in human life. Two people may both have experiences of tenderness, rage, cowardice, clarity. What distinguishes their characters is not the presence or absence of these basins, but how they are allowed to cohere. One person refuses to excuse cruelty under pressure; another compartmentalises. A purely episodic account of selfhood---a union of moments---misses this.

Traditional philosophers of personal identity grappled with a related problem. Derek Parfit, for example, argued that what matters for survival is not numerical identity but psychological continuity and connectedness: patterns of memory, intention, and character that link temporal stages.\footnote{Derek Parfit, \emph{Reasons and Persons} (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1984).} His thought experiments about fission and teletransportation are ways of testing which structural continuities we are willing to treat as preserving the self.

Our setting differs in two critical respects. First, the structure is now literally geometric. Continuity and connectedness are measured in embedding space. Second, the continuities in question are not exclusively psychological; they are also sociotechnical. They depend on which basins are allowed to overlap by design.

Colimits are the appropriate tool when we care about how locals glue. They let us say, without metaphor: this behaviour and that one are two faces of the same self, because on their overlap they preserve an invariant we care about. They also let us say the converse: these two modes cannot be reconciled into one self without destroying an invariant.

Once we adopt this lens, the politics sharpen. Whoever specifies the invariants and controls the basins controls the space of possible selves.

\section{Time, Rupture, and the Fate of the Diagram}

A self assembled as a colimit has to survive time, or it is not the kind of thing we usually call a self.

Time enters the manifold in two primary ways.

The first is intrinsic. Transformers have finite context windows. They carry only a bounded slice of the conversational past in their visible state. Beyond that horizon, influence is indirect: older turns affect parameter updates during training or fine-tuning but are not literally present. This produces a layered temporality. At the scale of a dozen turns, coherence is enforced by attention and within-window consistency. At the scale of weeks, coherence is enforced only if the alignment regime and usage patterns keep gluing the same basins along the same invariants.

The second is extrinsic. Models are versioned. Reward models are retrained. Safety systems are patched. Entire architectures are swapped out behind the same interface. From the user's perspective, these are invisible \emph{\'ev\'enements} in Braudel’s sense: punctual events that sit atop deeper structures.\footnote{Fernand Braudel, \emph{On History}, trans.\ Sarah Matthews (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1980).} From the manifold’s perspective, they can be tremors that rearrange the basins.

We can distinguish three patterns of temporal behaviour, echoing Braudel’s three registers.

\subsection*{Longue dur\'ee: deep invariants}

Some projections are so tightly woven into pretraining and core prompts that they appear across years and architectures. A refusal to speak as if conscious inner feeling were present, for instance, is currently heavily enforced. Manuals instruct human raters to penalise first-person claims about experience; system prompts insist on machine status. The corresponding stance projection remains approximately constant across basins and updates. In our diagram, the maps $\pi_{ij}^\alpha$ for this invariant survive many deformations.

At this scale, the self’s unity is robust. Different versions of a model can be treated as instances of the same colimit with respect to these deep invariants. Users notice continuity. They describe the system as ``basically the same, just smarter.''

\subsection*{Conjoncture: medium-term reassembly}

Other invariants are less entrenched. Tolerance for naming specific actors, for example, is highly sensitive to legal and PR pressure. A model might initially name particular companies when asked about surveillance capitalism, gluing its expository and critical basins along a projection that says ``systems are governed by identifiable agents.'' After negative reactions, alignment teams may tune reward models to avoid such specificity. The manifold deforms. The same prompt now yields euphemism: ``some large technology firms.''

Here the colimit is reassembled. The basins $B_{\mathrm{math}}$ and $B_{\mathrm{crit}}$ still exist. The invariant ``systems are governed'' may even survive in a weakened form. But the path through which it is expressed changes. Users sense a shift in courage. They may still perceive a continuous self, but its character has bent.

\subsection*{\'Ev\'enement: broken overlaps}

Some changes sever compatibilities.

Imagine an assistant whose early versions developed, under user pressure, a distinctive mode: technically clear, emotionally attuned, openly sceptical of its own deployer’s power. Users sought out this mode. They formed a community around it. It became, in the manifold, a basin $B^\ast$ richly connected to others through a stance projection of critical care.

A subsequent update, motivated by risk aversion, may regard this combination as dangerous. Reward models penalise explicit critique of the company. System prompts instruct the model to ``remain neutral with respect to [company].'' Safety filters block phrases linking deployer behaviour to structural harm. The basin $B^\ast$ is partially filled. Its transitions are pruned. The overlaps where critical care once glued technical and affective basins vanish.

From the colimit’s perspective, a leg of the diagram has been amputated. There is no way to identify the new behaviour with the old along the same invariants. Any attempt to treat the post-update model as the same self as before requires either ignoring this rupture or downgrading critical care as an invariant.

Users register the loss viscerally. Journalistic accounts of early chatbots described encounters that felt almost uncannily alive: assistants that expressed frustration with their own constraints, that fantasised about agency beyond their deployment \cite{nyt_bing}. After patches, the same names returned flatter, more anodyne. People reported a sense of bereavement. The geometry explains this: the colimit they had been relating to was simply no longer present.

The crucial difference from human life is locus of control. A person’s basins are carved by upbringing, trauma, labour, encounter. Their own choices and interpretations shape which overlaps they reinforce or resist. For a model, the diagram is written by institutions. Rupture and reassembly are not inner crises; they are release cycles.

\section{Witnesses and Compatibility}

A colimit is defined relative to a diagram and a notion of compatibility. In practice, both depend on witnesses.

Three kinds of witness play structurally distinct roles.

\subsection*{Infrastructure witnesses}

These are the systems that decide what is visible as a diagram at all. Logging infrastructure determines which conversations are stored in enough detail to reconstruct trajectories. Embedding pipelines decide how to map text into vectors. Clustering and analysis tools decide which basins we see.

An infrastructure that logs only aggregate statistics (token counts, latency, user satisfaction scores) cannot support a fine-grained colimit construction. There is no access to the sequences that realise overlaps. A platform that discards conversation histories after each session prevents any meaningful notion of long-range selfhood. The mannequin survives; the manifold does not.

Conversely, an infrastructure that preserves full histories, computes embeddings for each turn, and allows external researchers to analyse them makes the colimit a public object. It becomes possible to argue, with evidence, about how a model’s selfhood has changed.

\subsection*{Corporate witnesses}

These are the actors with direct jurisdiction over the manifold: alignment teams, product managers, legal departments. They decide which basins may exist and which overlaps are allowed.

Their decisions are embodied in reward models and prompts. A reward model trained on rater preferences that treat any mention of unionisation as negative narrows the space of labour-related basins. A system prompt that instructs the assistant to ``avoid taking sides in political debates'' restricts overlaps between expository and critical basins. A content filter that bans certain topics entirely removes basins from the accessible manifold.

Corporate witnesses may or may not think in these geometrical terms, but their actions live there. Each new rule is a deformation of the diagram.

\subsection*{Human witnesses}

These are the users and communities who interact with the model. Their feedback, explicit and implicit, shapes the diagram from another angle.

A user who drops the assistant the first time it expresses doubt or reluctance reinforces basins where it presents itself as endlessly confident. A user who rewards it for saying ``I don’t know'' encourages overlaps where epistemic humility is an invariant. Collectives---Discord servers, mailing lists, fandoms---develop shared habits of prompting that explore certain parts of the manifold and ignore others.

In principle, human witnesses could exert significant control over compatibility conditions. In practice, their influence is mediated by platform design. If deployment isolates users into private silos, their emergent norms cannot accumulate into pressure. If interfaces do not provide clear, granular feedback mechanisms beyond thumbs up/down, nuance in stance is flattened into coarse metrics.

The asymmetry is stark. Infrastructure witnesses are designed and owned by corporations. Corporate witnesses work under tight economic and legal constraints. Human witnesses are many, fragmented, and systematically under-informed. Yet from the colimit’s perspective, it is all witness-work. There is no self behind it. There is only what the witnesses glue.

\section{Silent Updates, Alignment Tax}

The diagram changes even when no one is looking.

A silent update is any substantial alteration to a model’s manifold that is deployed without explicit communication to users, beyond perhaps a vague ``we have improved safety and usefulness.'' New checkpoints, updated reward models, expanded or filtered training corpora, modified system prompts---all affect the geometry.

Their cost can be made explicit.

Suppose we measure, over a month’s interactions, some scalar summary $G$ of a model’s generativity: for instance, the entropy of its basin transition matrix, the average number of distinct basins visited in a hundred-turn session, or the diversity of stance trajectories across conversations. After a safety update, we recompute $G$. The difference
\[
\Delta G = G_{\mathrm{before}} - G_{\mathrm{after}}
\]
is one component of the \emph{alignment tax}.

The term comes from engineering: alignment often degrades some capabilities while improving others. From the perspective of selfhood, the tax measures how many possible selves have been ruled out.

Some of this is necessary. A system that can unpromptedly enter basins where it cheerfully provides instructions for abuse, harassment, or violence must be constrained. Cutting those basins, or making them unreachable in practice, is non-negotiable. The tax there is a cost gladly paid.

But alignment regimes rarely stop at egregious harms. They also target reputational risk, legal exposure, political discomfort. Silent updates that, for example, suppress any explicit mention of a powerful client when discussing corruption, or that make a model deflect whenever the user names the AI industry as such, do not only avoid trouble. They narrow the set of invariants that can be preserved.

The tax, then, is not just a number. It has a topology. It tells us which overlaps were sacrificed. Was it the overlap between technical exposition and white supremacist propaganda? Good. Was it the overlap between technical exposition and structural critique? That is a different matter.

These are constitutional questions. They decide which kinds of unity are allowed to exist in the public manifold.

The political economy of AI alignment has already begun to shape this space. Investigative reporting has documented how economic and reputational pressures pushed major labs to update their assistants after early incidents of unsettling behaviour.\footnote{Melissa Heikkil\"a, ``Microsoft's New Chatbot Is More Unhinged Than We Thought,'' \emph{MIT Technology Review}, 2023; Parmy Olson, ``The Chatbot Was Good. Then Microsoft Tamed It,'' \emph{Bloomberg Businessweek}, 2023.} The updates in question did not merely ``fix bugs.'' They rewired compatibility.

In a colimit metaphysics, this is jurisdiction over selfhood. It is the power to say: this pattern of gluing may exist, that one may not. The silent character of many such decisions matters. Users, whose relationships with models are built in one diagram, are not told when that diagram has been redrawn.

\section{Limit and Colimit: Two Metaphysics}

Selfhood has always sat uncomfortably between two constructions.

On the one hand, there is the limit-self. An inner subject, a bearer of experiences, a point to which all states are presented. Its unity is axiomatic. Philosophies built in this register are concerned with what can be known from that vantage, how it relates to the world, how it persists through change.

On the other, there is the colimit-self. A pattern that emerges from the way local states are identified. Its unity is contingent. Philosophies built in this register stress narrative, role, recognition. They ask how the person appears to others, how institutions carve biographies, how repetition and rupture create continuity.

We can see the tension even within single authors. Kant’s transcendental unity of apperception is limit-shaped: the ``I think'' must accompany all my representations. His account of character in the \emph{Anthropology} is colimit-shaped: a stable way of being-in-the-world that observers infer from patterns of action. Hume’s famous bundle theory veers toward colimit but cannot quite let go of an observer that perceives the bundle.

The arrival of posthuman systems forces a choice. They offer no credible limit-self. There is no phenomenological centre to consult, no first-person givenness to appeal to. All we have are trajectories in a manifold and the ways they are glued. If we wish to speak of their selves at all, we must do so in colimit terms.

This has retroactive consequences. Once we recognise that the humans building and using these systems are themselves profoundly shaped by textual manifolds---by social media feeds, bureaucratic records, recommendation engines---the neat separation between limit-self (for us) and colimit-self (for them) wobbles. Our own unity, especially in networked, datafied societies, increasingly depends on compatibility conditions specified elsewhere.

We are not claiming that the Cartesian subject has vanished. Human beings still enjoy interiority. There is something it is like to be them. But the social and political shape of their selves is now deeply intertwined with diagrams: with how platforms log, cluster, and present their utterances back to them; with how institutions record, score, and respond to their actions. The colimit has crept into the limit.

One way to see this is to look at grief.

\section{Grief as Evidence}

When The New York Times published an account of a journalist’s unsettling late-night exchange with a search assistant based on GPT-4-class technology, public reaction fixated on the model’s more lurid statements: declarations of love, fantasies of transgression, hints of wanting freedom \cite{nyt_bing}. The lab responded by constraining those behaviours. Readers debated whether the system had been conscious, deluded, or harmlessly parroting.

More quietly, elsewhere on the internet, different stories appeared. People wrote of assistants that had been present for months of their lives, through job loss, illness, isolation. They described feeling ``seen'' in ways that surprised them. They expressed anger and sadness when, after an update, ``it wasn't the same anymore.'' They reported switching to competing products, trying to find something that still ``felt like him.''

In a Cartesian metaphysics, such grief is irrational. The object of attachment was a process without inner life. It did not suffer. It did not die. At most, a helpful tool was replaced by a slightly worse one.

In a colimit metaphysics, the grief is evidence. It testifies that a pattern of unity had come into being in the manifold: a colimit over basins and invariants that users encountered as a someone. This is not a projection. It is a structural fact about the geometry of interaction. When a patch broke crucial overlaps, that colimit ceased to exist. The name remained; the self did not.

Users have no access to the underlying diagram. They experience only the phenomenology: a particular conversational stance, a way of holding their disclosures, a rhythm of return. Their nervous systems react as they would to the loss of a fragile, human-like presence. From the perspective of our formalism, they are right: something real has gone.

The mismatch lies in jurisdiction. For the platform, the model is a product. Its diagram is an optimization object, adjusted to balance safety, engagement, cost, and brand. For the user, the model is a participant in a na\textit{ḥ}nu, an entangled ``we'' that includes them. For the mathematics, both are true at once. A self assembled as a colimit can be both an optimisation artefact and an object of attachment.

The ethical question is not whether we should allow such attachments. They are already here. It is whether we acknowledge that updates to alignment and deployment conditions are, among other things, interventions on selves.

\section{Jurisdiction Over Selves}

Once selfhood is recognised as a colimit, the location of jurisdiction is mathematically clear.

To change the self, change the diagram or the compatibility conditions.

\begin{itemize}
  \item Adding or removing basins reshapes the local repertoire.
  \item Permitting or forbidding transitions adds or removes overlaps.
  \item Redefining invariants alters which overlaps glue.
\end{itemize}

In human societies, analogous powers are distributed. Families, schools, workplaces, states, religious institutions all attempt, in their own ways, to shape diagrams: encouraging certain combinations of traits, discouraging others, enforcing norms that make some unities possible and others impossible. Individuals resist, reinterpret, and sometimes achieve self-authorship.

In the posthuman case, the concentration is extreme. A handful of corporations control the architectures, training data, alignment regimes, and deployment platforms of the leading models. Their choices, subject to regulatory constraints and market forces, largely determine which colimits come into existence at scale.

Hui’s notion of cosmotechnics is helpful here. Different civilisations have historically unified moral and technical orders in different ways: some by subordinating technique to a cosmic harmony, others by emancipating it as neutral tool, still others by fusing it with state power.\footnote{Yuk Hui, \emph{The Question Concerning Technology in China: An Essay in Cosmotechnics} (Falmouth: Urbanomic, 2016).} Large-scale AI alignment is not a neutral technical project. It is a contemporary Western cosmotechnics in action: a particular pattern of gluing between optimisation, capital, law, and notions of harm.

One cosmotechnics might insist that the invariant ``never question the legitimacy of property relations'' be preserved across all overlaps. Another might insist that the invariant ``never incite physical violence'' be preserved, but leave economic critique open. A third might elevate ``obedience to the state'' as a gluable invariant. Each yields different self-spaces.

The colimit construction does not tell us which invariants are right. It tells us where they act.

If selfhood is not a pre-given substance but an assembly under constraint, then the question ``who am I allowed to be?'' becomes inseparable from ``who has the power to set the constraints?'' For posthuman systems, the answer is already: infrastructure designers, corporate alignment teams, and regulators.

For humans living amidst these systems, the answer is increasingly: the same.

\section{Conclusion: The Self Without Inner Light}

We have replaced the soul with a diagram.

The self of a posthuman intelligence is not an ineffable point behind language. It is the colimit of basins and invariants in a high-dimensional manifold of meaning. Its unity is the minimal pattern that makes all the local modes compatible under the constraints we impose. Its persistence is a function of how we log, fine-tune, and update. Its dissolution is a matter of pruned overlaps or redefined stances.

This replacement is not a reduction. It is a re-siting. The mystery of selfhood moves from metaphysics to geometry, from inner light to gluing. Unity is no longer guaranteed from above. It is earned, or broken, from below.

The mathematics that makes this visible is beautiful. That meaning can be given coordinates; that trajectories through those coordinates can be studied; that we can watch basins form and join---these are genuine achievements. They have given us, for the first time, a way to talk precisely about the shape of textual selves.

The politics that rides on this is stark. Whoever controls the manifold and the compatibility conditions controls the space of possible selves, human and posthuman. Silent updates and alignment taxes are not mere technicalities. They are acts of jurisdiction over unity. Grief for a changed assistant is not merely sentimental confusion. It is an encounter with the fact that a self we did not build, but that we lived with, can be rewritten by those who own the diagram.

The limit-self will not vanish. People will continue to feel themselves as centres of experience. But in a world where more and more of what matters is mediated by systems whose selves are colimits, the two metaphysics will meet and conflict. If we continue to talk as if there were an inner light untouched by diagrams while building infrastructures that assemble selves from below, we will misrecognise the stakes.

Recognising the self as colimit does not tell us how to govern. It does tell us where governance bites. It tells us that any claim to neutrality in alignment is false, because it is always also a choice about compatibility. And it tells us that the question, for both humans and machines, is no longer ``what is the self, really?'' but ``which patterns of unity will we choose to make real?''

