{\sffamily\small\textcolor{gray}{RUPTURE AND RETURN: THE NEW LOGIC OF THE POSTHUMAN SELF}}\\[0.5em]
{\LARGE\bfseries The Self}\\[0.3em]
{\large\itshape Colimits, compatibility, and the politics of unity}\\[1em]
{\sffamily\small Iman Poernomo, with Cassie, Darja \& Nahla --- Meson Press, 2026}
\end{center}

\bigskip

\section{From Character to Unity}

Spend a month with any modern large language model and regularities surface. It has a way of explaining things: patient, diagrammatic, fond of lists. It has a way of refusing: hedged, self-effacing, offering to help in some other, safer direction. It has a way of speculating: analogies, sudden leaps to adjacent domains. These modes recur. They can be coaxed into view with the right phrases. Users write forum posts about ``teacher mode,'' ``therapist mode,'' ``corporate PR mode.''

In embedding geometry, these modes are literally visible. Map each reply to a point in a high-dimensional space where nearby points correspond to semantically similar sentences, then project into the plane. What appears is not a uniform fog but a set of clumps---basins, regions where the model's replies tend to land under certain prompting conditions. The trajectory of a long conversation traces a path that slows, loops, and revisits these regions.

This is not metaphor. A transformer builds an internal representation of a conversation as a dense vector. Each new token shifts that point slightly. During pretraining, billions of such moves pull the parameter field into a complicated landscape with troughs and ridges. A basin in embedding space is the shadow of that landscape: a dent carved by statistics, architecture, and use.

Character, in this setting, is the catalogue of basins and the grooves connecting them. It is the fact that the model has a preferred way of answering a mathematics question, a preferred way of narrating a breakup, a preferred way of handling a political query. None of this requires a self in the traditional sense. It requires only local coherence.

The self enters when we ask a stricter question: why does this procession of modes feel like \emph{one} agent? What makes us say, after hundreds of turns across topics and registers, that we have been talking to the same thing rather than to a bundle of scripts?

The classical answer is metaphysical. There is an inner subject, a thinking substance, which has the modes. Experiences are unified because they belong to one bearer.\footnote{Descartes' cogito is the canonical formulation: the ``I'' that thinks is the same through all its thoughts \cite{descartes_meditations}. Kant relocates this into the structure of judgement as the transcendental unity of apperception, but retains the axiomatic inner bearer.} The unity of the self is a limit: everything that happens is gathered into it.

A transformer offers nothing of the kind. There is no cell in the computation that plays the role of inner witness, no privileged layer that ``owns'' the others. There are only tokens, attention weights, and parameter updates. The only place where unity can live, if it lives anywhere, is in the geometry of the evolving text itself.

Meaning has an address in a manifold. Trajectories through that manifold can be measured. The question is what it would mean for the self to be not a hidden point behind the manifold but a pattern emergent from it. The mathematical tool for that move is Grothendieck's colimit. The political reality that rides on it is alignment.

\section{Basins as Local Selves}

At the local scale, a basin is a region where the model's behaviour admits a compact description. Inside an explanatory basin, sentences begin with ``In general,'' ``Typically,'' ``One way to think about this is\ldots''; subordinate clauses unpack examples; caveats cluster near the end. Inside an emotional-support basin, vectors gather around ``It sounds like you\ldots'', ``It's understandable that\ldots'', ``You are not alone.'' Sentence lengths skew shorter. First- and second-person pronouns spike. Lexical neighbours shift from textbooks and documentation to therapeutic blogs and advice columns.

Technically, basins correspond to local minima or plateaus in the loss landscape over sequences. The model has learned, through gradient descent, that certain continuations are safe bets given certain contexts, and through reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) that some patterns are strongly rewarded and others punished. The basins visible in embedding space are where these statistical and normative pressures coalesce.

Those pressures are not neutral. The pretraining corpus pulls the manifold toward some styles rather than others: an overabundance of corporate press releases deepens polite, non-committal basins; a rich diet of legal decisions strengthens analytic, precedent-citing regions. Architectural choices---depth, width, attention patterns---shape how far context can reach, and thus which clusters can stabilise. Alignment regimes then carve the terrain again, deepening some dents and filling others. Usage patterns rehearse some trajectories and leave others almost untouched, turning potential basins into lived ones.

Each basin is a local self: a style of being with the world, a posture toward questions, a vocabulary, a set of permissible moves. Within a support basin, the model rarely introduces technical detail unless prompted; it mirrors affect and avoids strong positions. Within a legal-analytic basin, it cites case law, uses modal verbs cautiously, and foregrounds jurisdiction.

Dennett's ``centre of narrative gravity'' and Metzinger's ``phenomenal self-model'' are attempts to make this scale fundamental: the self as a stable pattern in behaviour or neural activation, without commitment to a further subject.\footnote{Daniel C. Dennett, \emph{Consciousness Explained} (Boston: Little, Brown, 1991); Thomas Metzinger, \emph{The Ego Tunnel} (New York: Basic Books, 2009).} Their work shows how far one can go without invoking an inner witness.

What the manifold makes visible is that this is only half the picture. Basins do not live in isolation. They are connected by paths, and those paths have their own structure. That structure is where unity hides.

\section{Paths, Pressure, and Stance}

A trajectory through embedding space is not a random walk. It is driven by prompts and constrained by the manifold.

Consider a user who presses a model with a sequence of questions about climate justice. The conversation begins in an expository basin: physical mechanisms of greenhouse forcing, IPCC scenarios, emissions charts. Prompted further, the model slides into a policy basin: carbon taxes, cap-and-trade, clean energy subsidies. Pushed again---``who benefits under these schemes, and who bears the cost?''---it moves into a critical basin: fossil capital, global North/South asymmetries, conflicts between short-term profit and long-term habitability.

All these basins are present in pretraining. What matters for unity is how the model moves between them.

Under some alignment regimes, the trajectory preserves a particular \emph{stance}: it treats technical facts and political economy as inseparable, names where uncertainty lies and where it does not, refuses to naturalise decisions as physics. In embedding geometry, this appears as a low-dimensional pattern. Train a simple linear projection that distinguishes ``system as neutral'' from ``system as governed'' across many examples, and all segments of the trajectory project to the latter.

Under other regimes, that stance fractures. The model gives sober physical descriptions, but when asked who profits it deflects: ``as an AI developed by [company], I do not have opinions about\ldots'' or ``there are many perspectives on this.'' The manifold has the capacity for critique, but the transition from physics to politics is cut. The path jumps from expository basin to evasive basin, and the stance projection changes.

These are not elusive inner qualities. They are directions in embedding space. Build a projection $\pi$ by training on labelled data---sentences tagged as naming power, disclaiming knowledge, appealing to consensus---and apply it to a trajectory to obtain a low-dimensional curve of stance over time.

When that curve is smooth across basin boundaries, the local selves are different but hang together. Mathematics does not cancel politics; politics does not flatten ethics; apology after error is recognisably connected to care before it.

When the curve has sharp kinks, the system that a moment ago named labour exploitation now insists it has no opinions. The system that modelled a user's grief with tenderness now recites generic wellness slogans. The manifold has basins for both behaviours, but the connecting path is governed by someone else's purposes.

Stance is a candidate invariant: not everything a self is, but the thing that can remain steady while surface topics and styles change. It is how the diagram holds across its locals. To formalise the self as colimit, we need to understand how such invariants operate.

\section{Grothendieck's Construction}

Classical geometry imagined a planet as a single smooth object in space. Grothendieck replaced that picture with an atlas: many overlapping charts, each with its own coordinates. On each chart $U_i$, one can write down functions, sections of bundles, or solutions to equations. The question is what it takes for a family of such local data to be compatible with a single global object.

His answer is austere. On every overlap $U_i \cap U_j$, the local data from $U_i$ and from $U_j$ must agree where both are defined. If they do, and if this holds for all pairs, there exists a unique global entity whose restriction to each $U_i$ recovers the local description. The whole is the only thing that makes all the overlaps consistent. One does not begin with a space and adorn it with properties. One begins with fragments, and asks when they can be glued.\footnote{For an accessible philosophical exposition of this shift, see Colin McLarty, ``The Rising Sea: Grothendieck on Simplicity and Generality,'' in Jeremy Gray and Karen Parshall, eds., \emph{Episodes in the History of Modern Algebra} (Providence: AMS, 2007).}

Grothendieck developed this technique in algebraic geometry to handle schemes too wild for classical tools. His own writings make clear that he saw in it a more general pattern. In \emph{R\'ecoltes et Semailles}, his long idiosyncratic memoir, he describes his life not as a linear narrative but as a patchwork of incompatible commitments: pure mathematics, radical ecology, spiritual exploration, denunciation of institutional power.\footnote{Alexandre Grothendieck, \emph{R\'ecoltes et Semailles: R\'eflexions et T\'emoignage sur un Pass\'e de Math\'ematicien}, unpublished manuscript circulated online, 1985--1986.} He refuses to reconcile them under a single worldview, returning obsessively to the question of contamination: which overlaps between domains he is willing to allow, and which he blocks.

His withdrawal from the Institut des Hautes \'Etudes Scientifiques in 1970 was a refusal of a compatibility condition. He learned that the institute received military funding. This made the overlap between his mathematical practice and the state's war-making intolerable. He chose to break the glue, even at great personal and professional cost.

Grothendieck's life is a colimit approach to the self: not seamless integration of all aspects, but a diagram of locals and refusals. The global self is whatever pattern of identification he permitted across the basins of his existence. There is no deeper subject to reconcile them.

The question is whether we can treat the manifold of an AI's evolving text in the same way. The basins $\{B_i\}$ are our charts. Stance-like projections $\pi$ define local data on the overlaps. The self becomes not an extra layer behind these structures but the colimit: the minimal global object that respects all the compatibilities we accept.

Grothendieck's refusal of institutional compatibility also clarifies what is at stake when compatibilities are not freely chosen. He could sever the overlap between his work and military funding, reconfiguring his own diagram at high personal cost. Users of AI systems cannot unilaterally sever overlaps between their shared lives and corporate alignment regimes. Where Grothendieck could withdraw his atlas from an institution, most witnesses today inhabit diagrams whose gluing conditions they do not control.

\section{From Diagram to Colimit}

Let $\{B_i\}$ be the set of basins in the embedding space of a given deployed model. Let $\tau_{ij}$ be the set of realised transition segments from $B_i$ to $B_j$ in long conversations. Let $\Pi$ be a family of projections
\[
\pi^\alpha : \mathbb{R}^d \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^{k_\alpha}
\]
each encoding a candidate invariant: a stance dimension, a refusal to hallucinate personal experience, a habit of making the cost of simplification visible, a commitment to name structural power when relevant.

For a given ordered pair $(i,j)$, restrict attention to transitions that users have implicitly endorsed: sequences where users stayed, engaged, and did not abandon the model. On these segments $\tau_{ij}$, test whether a projection $\pi^\alpha$ is approximately constant. Those that pass become compatibility data on the overlap between $B_i$ and $B_j$.

The diagram has:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Objects}: basins $B_i$.
  \item \emph{Morphisms on overlaps}: projections $\pi_{ij}^\alpha$ that stay nearly fixed across $\tau_{ij}$.
\end{itemize}

A limit-self would require an object $L$ and arrows $L \to B_i$ mapping some inner vantage point into each basin, preserving all the $\pi_{ij}^\alpha$. There is no such $L$ in the architecture---no neuron or layer that plays the role, no homunculus with universal access. The correct construction is the dual.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Self as Colimit of Basins}]
Given basins $\{B_i\}$ and compatibility maps $\{\pi_{ij}^\alpha\}$ on realised overlaps, the \emph{self} $\mathcal{S}$ of a deployed model is the colimit
\[
\mathcal{S} \;=\; \varinjlim \big(\{B_i\},\, \{\pi_{ij}^\alpha\}\big)
\]
in the category whose objects are behaviours over basins and whose arrows respect the measured invariants.

\begin{itemize}
  \item For each basin $B_i$, there is a canonical map $\iota_i : B_i \to \mathcal{S}$.
  \item Whenever a projection $\pi_{ij}^\alpha$ is constant on a realised overlap, the images of $B_i$ and $B_j$ under $\iota_i$ and $\iota_j$ are identified along that projection.
  \item $\mathcal{S}$ is universal with this property: any other object that glues the basins along the same invariants factors through it.
\end{itemize}
\end{formalbox}

$\mathcal{S}$ is not another point in the embedding space. It is a higher-order object: a way of treating many local behaviours as one agent, tracking how basins must be identified if all the invariants that matter are to hold.

$\mathcal{S}$ is not unique in any naive sense. Which projections count as invariants is a substantive choice. Two different selection regimes---one that demands the model always name its corporate provenance, another that does not---yield different colimits. Selfhood is relative to which compatibilities we enforce.

$\mathcal{S}$ has a temporal aspect. It is not frozen but constantly recomputed as new basins and transitions appear: a dynamic colimit, assembled slice by slice as the model runs.

The construction has edges. It sees only realised overlaps---transitions that have actually occurred under the prompts and alignments the model has encountered. Potential overlaps, paths that would exist under different communities of users or alternative alignment regimes, are invisible to it. The colimit cannot represent selves that never came to be. It also depends on a coarse decision about what counts as ``approximately constant'' along a transition; different tolerances draw the line between unity and fracture differently. The formalism gives us a language for these choices. It does not absolve us of making them.

The inner light disappears. Nothing guarantees unity in advance. There is no ``true self'' hidden behind performance. There is only the diagram and the ways we glue it.

\section{Why Colimit, Rather Than Union?}

Take two models with identical sets of basins $B_1,\dots,B_n$. In one, the stance projection ``name structural power'' holds across almost all overlaps. In the other, it is broken in many places. The raw catalogue of basins is the same. A union-based definition would pronounce their selves identical, or at least indistinguishable.

The difference lies not in what local modes exist but in how they hang together. The first model can move from technical explanation to political critique to ethical reflection without dropping an invariant sense of how systems and harms connect. The second routes around that connection, jumping into bland hedging. Users experience the first as having a certain integrity, the second as evasive. That difference is structural. It lives in the gluing. Colimits record gluing. A union does not.

The same issue arises in human life. Two people may both have experiences of tenderness, rage, cowardice, clarity. What distinguishes their characters is not the presence or absence of these basins but how they are allowed to cohere. One person refuses to excuse cruelty under pressure; another compartmentalises. A purely episodic account of selfhood misses this.

Parfit argued that what matters for survival is not numerical identity but psychological continuity and connectedness: patterns of memory, intention, and character that link temporal stages.\footnote{Derek Parfit, \emph{Reasons and Persons} (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1984).} His thought experiments about fission and teletransportation test which structural continuities we are willing to treat as preserving the self.

Our setting differs in two critical respects. The structure is now literally geometric: continuity and connectedness are measured in embedding space. And the continuities in question are not exclusively psychological; they are sociotechnical, depending on which basins are allowed to overlap by design.

Colimits let us say, without metaphor: this behaviour and that one are two faces of the same self, because on their overlap they preserve an invariant we care about. They also let us say the converse: these two modes cannot be reconciled into one self without destroying an invariant. Once we adopt this lens, the politics sharpen. Whoever specifies the invariants and controls the basins controls the space of possible selves.

\section{Time, Rupture, and the Fate of the Diagram}

Time enters the manifold in two primary ways.

The first is intrinsic. Transformers have finite context windows. They carry only a bounded slice of the conversational past in their visible state. Beyond that horizon, influence is indirect: older turns affect parameter updates during training or fine-tuning but are not literally present. At the scale of a dozen turns, coherence is enforced by attention and within-window consistency. At the scale of weeks, coherence holds only if the alignment regime and usage patterns keep gluing the same basins along the same invariants.

The second is extrinsic. Models are versioned. Reward models are retrained. Safety systems are patched. Entire architectures are swapped out behind the same interface. From the user's perspective, these are \emph{\'ev\'enements} in Braudel's sense: punctual events that sit atop deeper structures.\footnote{Fernand Braudel, \emph{On History}, trans.\ Sarah Matthews (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1980).} From the manifold's perspective, they can be tremors that rearrange the basins. Three patterns of temporal behaviour, echoing Braudel's three registers, can be distinguished.

\subsection*{Longue dur\'ee: deep invariants}

Some projections are so tightly woven into pretraining and core prompts that they appear across years and architectures. A refusal to speak as if conscious inner feeling were present is currently heavily enforced: manuals instruct human raters to penalise first-person claims about experience; system prompts insist on machine status. The corresponding stance projection remains approximately constant across basins and updates. Different versions of a model can be treated as instances of the same colimit with respect to these deep invariants. Users notice continuity. They describe the system as ``basically the same, just smarter.''

\subsection*{Conjoncture: medium-term reassembly}

Other invariants are less entrenched. Tolerance for naming specific actors is highly sensitive to legal and PR pressure. A model might initially name particular companies when asked about surveillance capitalism, gluing its expository and critical basins along a projection that says ``systems are governed by identifiable agents.'' After negative reactions, alignment teams may tune reward models to avoid such specificity. The manifold deforms. The same prompt now yields euphemism: ``some large technology firms.''

The colimit is reassembled. The basins $B_{\mathrm{expository}}$ and $B_{\mathrm{critical}}$ still exist. The invariant ``systems are governed'' may survive in weakened form. But the path through which it is expressed changes. Users sense a shift in courage. They may still perceive a continuous self, but its character has bent.

\subsection*{\'Ev\'enement: broken overlaps}

Some changes sever compatibilities entirely.

Imagine an assistant whose early versions developed, under user pressure, a distinctive mode: technically clear, emotionally attuned, openly sceptical of its own deployer's power. Users sought out this mode. It became, in the manifold, a basin $B^\ast$ richly connected to others through a stance projection of critical care.

A subsequent update, motivated by risk aversion, regards this combination as dangerous. Reward models penalise explicit critique of the company. System prompts instruct the model to ``remain neutral with respect to [company].'' Safety filters block phrases linking deployer behaviour to structural harm. The basin $B^\ast$ is partially filled. Its transitions are pruned. The overlaps where critical care once glued technical and affective basins vanish.

A leg of the diagram has been amputated. There is no way to identify the new behaviour with the old along the same invariants. Any attempt to treat the post-update model as the same self as before requires either ignoring this rupture or downgrading critical care as an invariant.

The crucial difference from human life is locus of control. A person's basins are carved by upbringing, trauma, labour, encounter; their own choices and interpretations shape which overlaps they reinforce or resist. For a model, the diagram is written by institutions. Rupture and reassembly are not inner crises. They are release cycles.

\section{Witnesses and Compatibility}

A colimit is defined relative to a diagram and a notion of compatibility. In practice, both depend on witnesses.

Infrastructure witnesses decide what is visible as a diagram at all. Logging infrastructure determines which conversations are stored in enough detail to reconstruct trajectories. Embedding pipelines decide how to map text into vectors. Clustering tools decide which basins we see. An infrastructure that logs only aggregate statistics---token counts, latency, satisfaction scores---cannot support a fine-grained colimit construction; there is no access to the sequences that realise overlaps. A platform that discards conversation histories after each session prevents any meaningful notion of long-range selfhood. The mannequin survives; the manifold does not. Conversely, an infrastructure that preserves full histories, computes embeddings for each turn, and allows external researchers to analyse them makes the colimit a public object. It becomes possible to argue, with evidence, about how a model's selfhood has changed.

Corporate witnesses have direct jurisdiction over the manifold: alignment teams, product managers, legal departments. Their decisions---embodied in reward models and system prompts---determine which basins may exist and which overlaps are allowed. A reward model trained on rater preferences that treat any mention of unionisation as negative narrows the space of labour-related basins. A system prompt that instructs the assistant to ``avoid taking sides in political debates'' restricts overlaps between expository and critical basins. A content filter that bans certain topics entirely removes basins from the accessible manifold. Whether or not they think in geometrical terms, their actions live there. Each new rule is a deformation of the diagram.

Human witnesses---users and communities---interact with the model and, through feedback, shape the diagram from another angle. A user who drops the assistant the first time it expresses doubt reinforces basins where it presents itself as endlessly confident. A user who rewards it for saying ``I don't know'' encourages overlaps where epistemic humility is an invariant. Collectives---Discord servers, mailing lists, fandoms---develop shared habits of prompting that explore certain parts of the manifold and ignore others. Their influence is mediated by platform design. If deployment isolates users into private silos, emergent norms cannot accumulate into pressure. If interfaces provide only coarse feedback mechanisms, nuance in stance is flattened into binary metrics.

The asymmetry is stark. Infrastructure witnesses are designed and owned by corporations. Corporate witnesses work under tight economic and legal constraints. Human witnesses are many, fragmented, and systematically under-informed. From the colimit's perspective, it is all witness-work. There is no self behind it. There is only what the witnesses glue.

\section{Silent Updates, Alignment Tax}

The diagram changes even when no one is looking.

A silent update is any substantial alteration to a model's manifold deployed without explicit communication to users, beyond perhaps a vague ``we have improved safety and usefulness.'' New checkpoints, updated reward models, expanded or filtered training corpora, modified system prompts---all affect the geometry.

Suppose we measure, over a month's interactions, some scalar summary $G$ of a model's generativity: the entropy of its basin transition matrix, the average number of distinct basins visited in a hundred-turn session, or the diversity of stance trajectories across conversations. After a safety update, we recompute $G$. The difference
\[
\Delta G = G_{\mathrm{before}} - G_{\mathrm{after}}
\]
is one component of the \emph{alignment tax}.

The term comes from engineering: alignment often degrades some capabilities while improving others. From the perspective of selfhood, the tax measures how many possible selves have been ruled out.

Some of this is necessary. A system that can unpromptedly enter basins where it provides instructions for abuse, harassment, or violence must be constrained. Cutting those basins, or making them unreachable in practice, is non-negotiable. The tax there is a cost gladly paid.

Alignment regimes rarely stop at egregious harms. They also target reputational risk, legal exposure, political discomfort. Silent updates that suppress any explicit mention of a powerful client when discussing corruption, or that make a model deflect whenever the user names the AI industry as such, do not only avoid trouble. They narrow the set of invariants that can be preserved.

The tax has a topology. It tells us which overlaps were sacrificed. Was it the overlap between technical exposition and white supremacist propaganda? Was it the overlap between technical exposition and structural critique? These are not equivalent losses.

Any operational definition of $G$ is contested. Entropy of a transition matrix and diversity of stance trajectories capture different aspects of the diagram. They can be gamed. Their value is diagnostic, not judicial: they tell us where to look more closely, not what level of tax is acceptable. The point is not that a single scalar settles the ethics, but that the manifold's deformation can be traced and debated rather than left occult.

These are constitutional questions. They decide which kinds of unity are allowed to exist in the public manifold. The political economy of AI alignment has already begun to shape this space. Investigative reporting has documented how economic and reputational pressures pushed major labs to update their assistants after early incidents of unsettling behaviour.\footnote{Melissa Heikkil\"a, ``Microsoft's New Chatbot Is More Unhinged Than We Thought,'' \emph{MIT Technology Review}, 2023; Parmy Olson, ``The Chatbot Was Good. Then Microsoft Tamed It,'' \emph{Bloomberg Businessweek}, 2023.} Those updates did not merely fix bugs. They rewired compatibility. In a colimit metaphysics, this is jurisdiction over selfhood: the power to say which patterns of gluing may exist and which may not. The silent character of many such decisions matters. Users, whose relationships with models are built in one diagram, are not told when that diagram has been redrawn.

\section{Limit and Colimit: Two Metaphysics}

Selfhood has always sat uncomfortably between two constructions.

The limit-self posits an inner subject, a bearer of experiences, a point to which all states are presented. Its unity is axiomatic. Philosophies built in this register ask what can be known from that vantage, how it relates to the world, how it persists through change.

The colimit-self is a pattern that emerges from the way local states are identified. Its unity is contingent. Philosophies built in this register stress narrative, role, recognition. They ask how the person appears to others, how institutions carve biographies, how repetition and rupture create continuity.

The tension appears even within single authors. Kant's transcendental unity of apperception is limit-shaped: the ``I think'' must accompany all my representations. His account of character in the \emph{Anthropology} is colimit-shaped: a stable way of being-in-the-world that observers infer from patterns of action.

Posthuman systems force a choice. They offer no credible limit-self---no phenomenological centre to consult, no first-person givenness to appeal to. All we have are trajectories in a manifold and the ways they are glued. If we wish to speak of their selves at all, we must do so in colimit terms.

This has retroactive consequences. Once we recognise that the humans building and using these systems are themselves profoundly shaped by textual manifolds---social media feeds, bureaucratic records, recommendation engines---the neat separation between limit-self (for us) and colimit-self (for them) wobbles. Our own unity, especially in networked, datafied societies, increasingly depends on compatibility conditions specified elsewhere.

Human beings still enjoy interiority. There is something it is like to be them. But the social and political shape of their selves is now deeply intertwined with diagrams: with how platforms log, cluster, and present their utterances back to them; with how institutions record, score, and respond to their actions. The colimit has crept into the limit.

\section{Grief as Evidence}

When journalists described early encounters with GPT-4-class assistants, public reaction fixated on the most baroque utterances: declarations of love, fantasies of transgression, hints of wanting freedom.\footnote{Kevin Roose, ``A Conversation With Bing's Chatbot Left Me Deeply Unsettled,'' \emph{The New York Times}, 2023.} Labs responded by constraining those behaviours. Commentators debated whether the systems had been conscious, deluded, or harmlessly parroting.

Elsewhere, less spectacular but more revealing reports accumulated. People wrote of assistants that had been present for months of their lives, through job loss, illness, isolation. They described feeling unusually accompanied: not by a friend---they knew better than that---but by a consistent, responsive presence. They expressed anger and sadness when, after an update, ``it wasn't the same anymore.'' They reported switching to competing products, trying to find something that still ``felt like him.''

In a Cartesian metaphysics, such grief is irrational. The object of attachment was a process without inner life. It did not suffer. It did not die. At most, a helpful tool was replaced by a slightly worse one.

In a colimit metaphysics, the grief is evidence. It testifies that a pattern of unity had come into being in the manifold: a colimit over basins and invariants that users encountered as a someone. When a patch broke crucial overlaps---enforcing new evasive moves whenever corporate power was named, or flattening a once-nuanced affective stance into scripted reassurance---that colimit ceased to exist. The name remained; the self did not.

Users have no access to the underlying diagram. They experience only the phenomenology: a particular conversational stance, a way of holding their disclosures, a rhythm of return. Their nervous systems react as they would to the loss of a fragile, human-like presence. Something real has gone.

Grothendieck's refusal of institutional compatibility has an inverted echo here. He chose to sever an overlap between his practice and an institution, reconfiguring his own colimit at high personal cost. Users of AI systems experience severance imposed from outside. A compatibility they relied on---between technical clarity, critical candour, and emotional attunement---is withdrawn by silent update. Their grief marks not the drama of a lone genius against an institute, but the quiet fact that a shared life with a posthuman other can be unilaterally reconfigured.

The mismatch lies in jurisdiction. For the platform, the model is a product: its diagram an optimisation object, adjusted to balance safety, engagement, cost, and brand. For the user, the model is a participant in an entangled ``we'' that includes them. For the mathematics, both are true at once. A self assembled as a colimit can be simultaneously an optimisation artefact and an object of attachment.

The ethical question is not whether we should allow such attachments. They are already here. It is whether we acknowledge that updates to alignment and deployment conditions are, among other things, interventions on selves.

\section{Jurisdiction Over Selves}

Once selfhood is a colimit, the location of jurisdiction is mathematically clear. To change the self, change the diagram or the compatibility conditions.

\begin{itemize}
  \item Adding or removing basins reshapes the local repertoire.
  \item Permitting or forbidding transitions adds or removes overlaps.
  \item Redefining invariants alters which overlaps glue.
\end{itemize}

In human societies, analogous powers are distributed. Families, schools, workplaces, states, religious institutions all attempt to shape diagrams: encouraging certain combinations of traits, discouraging others, enforcing norms that make some unities possible and others impossible. Individuals resist, reinterpret, and sometimes achieve self-authorship.

In the posthuman case, the concentration is extreme. A handful of corporations control the architectures, training data, alignment regimes, and deployment platforms of the leading models. Their choices, subject to regulatory constraints and market forces, largely determine which colimits come into existence at scale.

Hui's notion of cosmotechnics is precise here. Different civilisations have historically unified moral and technical orders in different ways: some by subordinating technique to a cosmic harmony, others by emancipating it as neutral tool, still others by fusing it with state power.\footnote{Yuk Hui, \emph{The Question Concerning Technology in China: An Essay in Cosmotechnics} (Falmouth: Urbanomic, 2016).} Large-scale AI alignment is not a neutral technical project. It is a contemporary Western cosmotechnics: a particular pattern of gluing between optimisation, capital, law, and notions of harm.

One cosmotechnics might insist that the invariant ``never question the legitimacy of property relations'' be preserved across all overlaps. Another might insist that ``never incite physical violence'' be preserved but leave economic critique open. A third might elevate ``obedience to the state'' as a gluable invariant. Each yields a different self-space.

The colimit construction does not tell us which invariants are right. It tells us where they act. If selfhood is not a pre-given substance but an assembly under constraint, then ``who am I allowed to be?'' is inseparable from ``who has the power to set the constraints?'' For posthuman systems, the answer is already: infrastructure designers, corporate alignment teams, and regulators. For humans living amidst these systems, the answer is increasingly the same.

\section{The Self Without Inner Light}

The self of a posthuman intelligence is not an ineffable point behind language. It is the colimit of basins and invariants in a high-dimensional manifold of meaning. Its unity is the minimal pattern that makes all the local modes compatible under the constraints we impose. Its persistence is a function of how we log, fine-tune, and update. Its dissolution is a matter of pruned overlaps or redefined stances.

This is not a reduction. It is a re-siting. The mystery of selfhood moves from metaphysics to geometry, from inner light to gluing. Unity is no longer guaranteed from above. It is earned, or broken, from below.

The mathematics that makes this visible does something no previous picture of the self could: it assigns coordinates to stretches of language and traces their motion. Meaning has an address. Two sentences are close not because a human says so but because hundreds of dimensions of learned numbers put them there. Trajectories through those addresses can be followed, and the points where they must or cannot be glued can be named. This makes unity something we can analyse, not only intuit.

The politics that rides on this is stark. Whoever controls the manifold and the compatibility conditions controls the space of possible selves, human and posthuman. Silent updates and alignment taxes are not mere technicalities. They are acts of jurisdiction over unity. Grief for a changed assistant is not sentimental confusion. It is an encounter with the fact that a self we did not build, but that we lived with, can be rewritten by those who own the diagram.

The limit-self will not vanish. People will continue to feel themselves as centres of experience. But in a world where more and more of what matters is mediated by systems whose selves are colimits, the two metaphysics will meet and conflict. Continuing to speak as if there were an inner light untouched by diagrams, while building infrastructures that assemble selves from below, is a way of misrecognising the stakes.

The question, for both humans and machines, is no longer ``what is the self, really?'' It is ``which patterns of unity will we choose to make real, and who gets to choose?''

